<% content_for :meta_title, "FPL Player Comparisons: Who Should I Pick? | Fantasy Forecast" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "Head-to-head FPL player comparisons, answered. Every pair graded A to F with expected points for the coming gameweek, from price, ownership, form and fixtures." %>
<% content_for :meta_url, "#{ApplicationHelper::BASE_URL}#{comparisons_path}" %>

<%# The heading says what the page is, in the words somebody would have typed to
    arrive at it. "Him or him?" is the better line, so it keeps its place underneath. %>
<%= render PageHeadingComponent.new(
      title: "FPL Player Comparisons#{" for Gameweek #{@gameweek.fpl_id}" if @gameweek}",
      subtitle: "Him or him?",
      share: "Share these FPL player comparisons",
      back: back_home) %>

<div class="mt-6">
  <%= render "comparisons/builder" %>
</div>

<p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm/6 text-zinc-600">
  The picks worth arguing about
  <%= @gameweek ? "for Gameweek #{@gameweek.fpl_id}" : "this season" %>: players our forecast puts
  next to each other, where the choice is genuinely close. Every pair has an address of its own,
  so the question can be asked the way you would ask it.
</p>

<% if @pairs.any? %>
  <% @pairs.each do |position, pairs| %>
    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-900"><%= position.capitalize %>s</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <% pairs.each do |pair| %>
          <li>
            <%= link_to comparison_title(pair), comparison_path(pair: pair.slug),
                class: "inline-block rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 px-3 py-1.5 text-sm text-zinc-700 hover:border-zinc-400 hover:text-zinc-950" %>
          </li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    </section>
  <% end %>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-900">How a comparison is answered</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-zinc-600">
      Both players are forecast the same way, from the same stored numbers the rankings are read from,
      so a comparison can never disagree with the tables. Price and ownership lead, weighed against
      minutes, recent form, expected goals and assists, and who they are playing. Where the gap is
      under <%= HeadToHead::CLOSE %> of a point a week we say so rather than invent a winner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%= render "shared/faq", title: "FPL Comparison FAQ", pairs: [
        [ "Which FPL player should I pick?",
          "Whichever of the two we forecast to score more, and every pair above says which that is. The comparison pages answer one question at a time: two players, the points we expect from each, and a verdict rather than a table for you to read yourself." ],
        [ "How do you decide who wins a comparison?",
          "Both players are forecast the same way, from the same stored numbers the rankings are read from, so a comparison can never disagree with the tables. Price and ownership lead, weighed against minutes, recent form, expected goals and assists, and who they are playing." ],
        [ "What if two players are too close to call?",
          "We say so. Where the gap is under #{HeadToHead::CLOSE} of a point a week it is smaller than the difference our forecast can honestly claim to see, so neither is named a winner. Owning either is the same decision." ],
        [ "Can I compare any two FPL players?",
          "Yes. Every pair of players in the game has an address of its own. The pairs listed here are the arguments worth having, the ones our own forecast puts closest together, and each of those pages links on to the next player worth weighing." ]
      ] %>
<% else %>
  <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-zinc-500">No forecasts yet for the coming gameweek, so there is nothing to compare.</p>
<% end %>
